<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Imperial Abbey of Marchtal</h1><p class="st-meta">1500 CE – 1803 CE (approx.) · capital Obermarchtal</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Premonstratensian abbey on the Danube in upper Swabia whose abbots ruled a small territory of villages until 1803.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>A collegiate foundation existed at Marchtal from the 8th century, and in 1171 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/count-palatine">Count Palatine</a> Hugh of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tubingen">Tübingen</a> refounded it for Premonstratensian canons. Raised to an abbey in 1440, it gained imperial immediacy around 1500, and its abbot sat on the Swabian bench of prelates.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The abbot governed several villages along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and in the hills nearby. Between 1686 and 1701 the canons built a large new church, designed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vorarlberg">Vorarlberg</a> masters Michael and Christian Thumb, one of the earliest great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> churches of upper Swabia. The abbey remained Catholic.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1803 the abbey was secularised and given to the princes of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-thurn-and-taxis">Thurn und Taxis</a>, who used the buildings as a residence. The territory passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-wurttemberg">Kingdom of Württemberg</a> in 1806, and the former abbey now belongs to the diocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocese-of-rottenburg-stuttgart">Rottenburg-Stuttgart</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="prince bishopric, 1500 CE – 1803 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M30 82 V46 Q50 12 70 46 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 30 V82 M40 58 H60"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">prince bishopric</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:89.55%;width:6.03%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000